"""Required illustrated-reading extensions following the preliminary AI review.
Audio source remains separately preserved; these sections are not represented as spoken.
"""
from html import escape as E

def panel(title, text):
 return '<section class="panel required-reading"><h2>'+title+'</h2>'+text+'</section>'
def table(headers, rows):
 return '<div class="table-wrap" role="region" aria-label="Worked example table" tabindex="0"><table><thead><tr>'+''.join('<th scope="col">'+E(x)+'</th>' for x in headers)+'</tr></thead><tbody>'+''.join('<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+E(x)+'</td>' for x in row)+'</tr>' for row in rows)+'</tbody></table></div>'
stages=panel('Required reading: name the qualification stages','''<p>URS and/or functional specification → DQ → applicable FAT/SAT → IQ → OQ → PQ. This is a learning map, not a mandatory identical sequence for every project. Document justified combinations and applicability.</p><p>DQ verifies the URS against the design. FAT/SAT examine supplier/site acceptance. IQ checks installation; OQ checks operation and limits; PQ checks intended performance. Keep the URS as the lifecycle reference (Annex 15 §§3.1–3.14).</p><p>For V-201, trace URS-02 through design drawing DR-02, applicable factory checks, installed sensor identification, operational accuracy testing, and performance evidence under the intended load. A supplier pass alone does not close every stage.</p>''')
additions={
2:panel('Required reading: VMP coverage','''<p>Annex 15 §1.4 says programme elements should be documented in a VMP or equivalent; the recorded phrase “may be defined” refers to the document choice, not an option to omit documentation.</p><p>Include policy, responsibilities, system/status summary, change and deviation handling, acceptance-criteria guidance, document references and strategy including applicable requalification (§1.5). Requalification evaluates continued control (§4).</p><p>Worked V-201 outline: policy—approved, risk-based qualification; owner—validation engineer with Quality oversight; status—DQ pending, installation not released; deviations—log and investigate failed criteria; changes—evaluate effects on URS and evidence; criteria—process owner provides scientific basis before approval; references—URS-V201, RA-V201, drawings; strategy—stage gates and periodic/change-triggered review of continued control. Each item needs an actual controlled reference in a real project.</p>''')+stages,
4:stages+panel('Worked artefact: a measurable URS','''<p>Supplemental worked-example assumption only: the base V-201 project has no supplied or approved process range or tolerance. For this separate fictional illustration, assume a process owner has provisionally proposed a 20–60 °C indication range and maximum absolute error of 0.5 °C in draft teaching document PB-02. These are not approved project criteria; site justification and authorization remain outstanding. These numbers are not general GMP tolerances.</p>'''+table(['Field','Filled teaching example'],[
('ID / need','URS-02: reliable temperature indication for V-201.'),
('Requirement','At steady reference temperatures of 20, 40 and 60 °C, the installed indication shall differ from the traceable reference by no more than ±0.5 °C at each point, in the approved test configuration.'),
('Verification basis','OQ-02 defines stabilization, reference uncertainty, repeat readings, sensor location and decision rule before execution. No averaging away an individual failure.'),
('Responsibility / status','Process owner must justify PB-02; validation drafts OQ-02; authorized site reviewers must approve both before execution. This is a proposed teaching requirement, not an approved specification.'),
('Boundary','This tests indication accuracy; it does not demonstrate mixing uniformity or product suitability.')])+'''<p>URS-01 remains a documentary material-suitability requirement: verification by records can be valid. URS-02 illustrates a separately measurable requirement. Reject “temperature shall be accurate” because it lacks conditions and a criterion.</p>'''),
5:stages+panel('Worked artefact: a traceability record',table(['Link','Filled teaching record'],[
('Requirement / design','Illustrative proposal only: URS-02 → draft PB-02 → DR-02 sensor and display specification. DQ-02 would check the design after the range and accuracy are justified and approved.'),
('Risk / stage','RA-02: misleading indication. FAT-02 may support design evidence; IQ-02 confirms installed sensor identity and calibration; OQ-02 tests the installed chain.'),
('Evidence / decision','In a hypothetical execution assuming approved ±0.5 °C criteria, OQ-02 row 3 gives +0.6 °C at 60 °C: numerical failure. DEV-02 would remain open. Base-project URS-02 remains unverified; no actual test occurred.'),
('Action / owner','If this were executed, instrument engineering would investigate; validation would repeat affected tests under approved deviation handling; Quality would review impact before closure.'),
('PQ boundary','PQ must address intended process performance. Passing OQ-02 alone does not establish product uniformity.')])+'''<p>For third-party protocols, site personnel assess suitability and compliance with internal procedures before approval (Annex 15 §2.6). Evaluate configuration, raw data, acceptance basis and transport/installation effects before reusing supplier results (§3.6).</p>'''),
6:panel('Required reading: risk vocabulary','''<p>Hazard: a potential source of harm. Harm: damage to health, including effects arising from lost product quality or availability. Risk combines the probability of harm and its severity. Severity describes how serious the consequence is; probability describes how likely it is. Detectability describes the ability to discover a hazard or failure. These are plain-language paraphrases of ICH Q9(R1) §7, not verbatim definitions.</p><p>V-201 example: inadequate mixing is the hazardous situation; stopped agitation is a failure mode; a drive fault is a possible cause; non-uniform concentration is a quality effect; incorrect dose and patient injury are possible downstream harms. The chain is conditional, not proof of a patient outcome. Uncertainty includes missing mixing studies and unknown failure frequency.</p><p>Distinguish the probability of equipment failure from the probability of resulting patient harm: intermediate controls and exposure matter.</p>'''),
7:panel('Worked artefact: FMEA with defined teaching scales','''<p>The following ordinal scales belong only to this fictional exercise. Numbers rank categories; multiplying them does not produce a measured probability or a calibrated amount of risk. Equal products can hide different severity. No automatic acceptance cutoff is used.</p>'''+table(['Score','Severity (S)','Occurrence (O)','Detection difficulty (D)'],[
('1','No expected quality effect in the stated scenario','Relevant evidence supports an infrequent failure','Independent verified detection prevents transfer'),
('2','Quality failure requiring batch investigation','Failure is plausible; limited relevant history','Detection depends on operator intervention'),
('3','Potential incorrect dose and patient harm','Repeated failures in relevant history','No effective detection before transfer')])+'''<p>Unknown frequency is marked uncertain; it must not silently receive O=1.</p>'''+table(['FMEA field','Filled teaching row'],[
('Failure / cause / effect','Agitator stops because a drive trips → concentration may vary → possible incorrect dose.'),
('Current controls / evidence','Operator observes speed display; the display does not prove uniformity. No relevant reliability history supplied.'),
('Scores / rationale','S=3 for potential dose harm; O=2 provisionally for a plausible failure with limited history; D=2 because operator response is required. Product=12, an ordinal prioritization aid only.'),
('Action / owner','Engineering proposes independent low-speed detection with transfer inhibition; validation challenges detection and response before reliance.'),
('Residual assessment','Pending effectiveness evidence. Do not lower S merely because an alarm is added. Reassess O/D using evidence; Quality reviews the remaining risk.'),
('Review trigger','New product, changed drive/control logic, an undetected stop, or new reliability evidence.')])+'''<p>Try the explorer using 3, 2, 2. Then compare 3×1×2 with 1×3×2: both equal 6, but the possible harm differs.</p>'''),
3:panel('Required reading: boundary and interface register','''<p>Worked teaching register for V-201. The vessel boundary does not eliminate interface dependencies. All references below are illustrative placeholders, not executed evidence.</p>'''+table(['Interface','Owner','Evidence to request / open decision'],[('Jacket utility to vessel','Engineering utilities','Utility qualification status and operating envelope; confirm that available supply supports intended temperature control.'),('Sensor to controller','Instrumentation','Sensor identification, calibration, control-loop design and fault-response challenge; do not infer independence from two displays.'),('Materials to product-contact surfaces','Production / Quality','Approved material suitability and contact compatibility records for the intended product family.'),('Vessel outlet to transfer hose','Production / Cleaning validation','Hose identity, cleaning verification and connection configuration; cleaning evidence remains open.')])+'''<p>Record each boundary, impact, document owner and outstanding action before assigning a qualification status.</p>'''),
8:panel('Required reading: a worked control plan','''<p>Illustrative scenario: V-201 agitation stops during preparation. A shared speed sensor can undermine both control and alarm functions, so independent protection must be demonstrated, not assumed.</p>'''+table(['Control-plan field','Worked teaching entry'],[('Risk and objective','Unnoticed loss of agitation could cause non-uniform concentration; prevent transfer until the consequence is assessed.'),('Proposed prevention / detection','Engineering evaluates drive reliability and an independently challenged low-speed signal; automatic transfer inhibition is proposed, not installed or verified.'),('Owner and due point','Engineering designs the control before OQ; validation prepares the fault challenge; production defines the batch-hold response; Quality reviews risk.'),('Verification and evidence','Approved OQ challenge of sensor failure, stopped drive, alarm annunciation and transfer inhibition; retain raw results, deviations and approved report.'),('Residual risk and trigger','Pending demonstrated effectiveness. Reassess after control-logic changes, a missed alarm, a drive trip or a new product; do not lower severity without rationale.')])+'''<p>Compare a shared-sensor alarm with a genuinely independent detection path. The number of controls is not proof of independence or effectiveness.</p>'''),
9:panel('Required reading: signed and absolute error','''<p>Each listed value is signed error: indication minus reference, in °C. All ten happen to be positive. The decision compares |error| with the illustrative limit; equality passes this numerical comparison. A −0.6 °C error would also exceed a 0.5 °C absolute limit.</p><p>The signed mean is +0.31 °C; range is 0.50 °C. Four absolute errors exceed 0.30 °C; one exceeds 0.50 °C. Measurement uncertainty and an approved decision rule are still needed for real conformity decisions. Ten observations do not establish capability or stability.</p>'''),
10:panel('Worked artefact: two different handover decisions','''<p>Additional fictional case facts: installation identification and electrical safety checks are complete. Transfer-hose cleaning evidence and product mixing range remain open. A separate, approved dry control-panel simulation uses no product, water or transfer hose. Its hazard review finds no interaction with those gaps. The responsible site reviewers have not yet signed its stage authorization.</p>'''+table(['Decision field','Filled teaching decision'],[
('Requested activity A','Routine oral-solution manufacture: NOT READY. Mixing and cleaning gaps affect intended use; 90% activity completion does not remove them.'),
('Requested activity B','Dry control-panel simulation: propose CONDITIONAL progression only, subject to documented no-significant-impact assessment and authorized stage release. This model is a recommendation, not permission.'),
('Restrictions','No product, no wetted operation, no transfer-hose use, no reliance on unverified alarm performance; follow approved simulation and safety controls.'),
('Open actions / owners','Process owner supplies mixing-range basis; cleaning specialist completes hose evidence; validation executes alarm challenge. Close affected evidence before routine-use review.'),
('Stop / reassess','If the simulation requires wet operation or relies on the unresolved control, stop and reassess. Conditional progression is then unsupported.'),
('Acceptance record','Signatures and dates: NOT PROVIDED. Annex 15 §2.10 permits justified conditional next-stage progression; it is not a blanket release for routine manufacture.')])+'''<p>Now change one fact: the proposed next activity uses the transfer hose. Explain why the same conditional decision no longer holds. A defensible answer names the specific activity, dependencies and authority—not merely “conditional”.</p>''')
}
# Scene-specific descriptions are based on the authored scene headings and labels; visual equivalence still requires diagram inspection.
scene_descriptions=[["The diagram connects patient need to the vessel's intended purpose and the evidence needed to support that purpose.","The three document roles are programme planning in a VMP, intended needs in a URS, and prioritization through risk assessment.","The three stages are defining needs, verifying them with evidence, and maintaining control after qualification.","The decision path identifies evidence gaps, assigns owners and requires resolution before routine use."],
["Policy, responsibilities and qualification strategy are separate elements of the programme map.","The scope groups facilities, utilities and equipment so interface ownership can be identified.","The VMP defines the programme approach, a protocol defines test methods, and a report evaluates findings.","A change initiates impact review and then a documented programme update; this is a review loop, not a completed qualification."],
["The boundary connects utility inlet, V-201 vessel and transfer outlet; each interface needs an owner.","The sensor feeds a controller that affects the product process; measurement faults can propagate through the control loop.","Engineering, operations and Quality oversight are distinct responsibilities for the same system.","The register compares instrument records, utility evidence and transfer cleaning; missing evidence remains open."],
["A vague adjective becomes a defined function and then a verification question.","A unique URS identifier links a clear statement to its acceptance basis.","Range, tolerance and test method are separate inputs; they remain unapproved in the base project.","A changed user need triggers impact assessment and an updated requirements baseline."],
["A requirement links to design response and test evidence; a missing link prevents closure.","Planned, executed and accepted are different test states; only reviewed evidence supports verification.","Supplier evidence requires source, applicability and integrity checks before site reliance.","A missing link creates an assigned action and needs reviewed closure before the requirement is marked verified."],
["The risk question asks what can fail, what harm may follow and under which conditions.","Equipment failure mode, quality effect and possible patient consequence are distinct parts of a conditional chain.","Known evidence, assumptions and missing knowledge are separated to expose uncertainty.","Cross-functional participants challenge assumptions and document a rationale rather than merely selecting a score."],
["Severity, occurrence and detection difficulty are three separate assessment dimensions.","Scores one, two and three represent ordered categories, not measured probabilities.","Two score combinations 3×1×2 and 1×3×2 both equal six, although their severities differ.","A proposed control requires effectiveness evidence before the residual risk is reassessed."],
["Prevention, detection and response are separate control functions; each needs a justified mechanism.","An action owner, verification method and due point are linked in the proposed control plan.","Adding a control may create a shared dependency; a common sensor fault can defeat controller and alarm.","Residual risk acceptance follows evidence review, recognition of limits and authorized decision-making."],
["Ten ordered observations form a small illustrative dataset without process context.","The example calculates mean +0.31 °C, range 0.50 °C and maximum +0.60 °C.","A numerical specification comparison differs from a trend signal or investigation trigger.","Ten points without representative conditions or a validated model cannot establish capability."],
["The review packet connects programme scope, traceability and open findings.","A 90% completion indicator contrasts with unresolved critical gaps and a pending decision.","A decision must state evidence, required actions and authorization, not merely a completion percentage.","The closing sequence plans coherently, verifies needs and revisits risk as new evidence emerges."]] 

def visual_description(i,j):
 return scene_descriptions[i-1][j]

new_questions=[
('V-201 has a purchase order but no agreed operating range. What should the team do before writing acceptance tests?', ['Use the supplier catalogue range as the process requirement','Agree intended use and justify the required operating range','Copy the range from the most recent vessel protocol'],1,'A supplier capability or another project does not establish this process need.'),
('A VMP lists protocols but gives no qualification status. Which change best supports the next-stage decision?', ['Add the equipment list again in an appendix','Replace status with percentage of budget spent','Link each system to status, evidence and open actions'],2,'Decision makers need evidence-linked status and remaining work.'),
('The jacket utility is outside the vessel boundary. Its availability affects temperature control. What belongs in the plan?', ['A utility interface owner and relevant qualification evidence','A statement that all outside-boundary systems are excluded','A second vessel test without checking utility conditions'],0,'Define interface dependencies and responsibilities rather than leaving a boundary gap.'),
('Which draft requirement can support a defined acceptance test?', ['Temperature indication should suit normal manufacturing','Temperature indication should meet the supplier standard','At defined reference points, indication error must be within ±0.5 °C'],2,'The last option is measurable, once the fictional basis, points and method are approved; ±0.5 °C is not a general GMP limit.'),
('The installed sensor differs from the sensor in a passing FAT report. How should URS verification be recorded?', ['Close it because the display model is unchanged','Keep it open while applicability and affected tests are assessed','Close it and schedule calibration after routine use starts'],1,'A changed measurement chain needs an applicability assessment and affected verification.'),
('An agitator trip may cause non-uniform concentration. Which statement describes a potential patient harm?', ['A patient receives an incorrect dose with an adverse effect','A drive fault causes the agitator to stop','A speed display fails to show the trip'],0,'Distinguish patient harm from equipment failure and detection failure.'),
('Two FMEA rows both score 6, but one has severity 3 and the other severity 1. What is defensible?', ['Accept both because equal products mean equal risk','Prioritize only the row with higher occurrence','Examine severity, evidence and controls before deciding'],2,'An ordinal product can conceal important differences.'),
('An alarm and controller share one sensor. Which evidence addresses the claimed protection?', ['A screenshot showing both functions enabled','A challenge of the shared fault and resulting response','Separate document numbers for the two functions'],1,'Functional testing must address the shared dependency, not merely documentation or configuration.'),
('A measured error is −0.6 °C and the illustrative absolute limit is 0.5 °C. What is the numerical result?', ['It exceeds the limit because its magnitude is 0.6 °C','It passes because −0.6 is less than +0.5','It passes if the signed average remains positive'],0,'Use absolute magnitude for a two-sided error limit; apply the approved uncertainty/decision rule in real testing.'),
('Hose cleaning evidence is missing, but a proposed dry simulation never uses the hose. What is the best next step?', ['Approve routine manufacture with a cleaning reminder','Reject every unrelated project activity automatically','Assess impact on that simulation and obtain stage authorization'],2,'Conditional progression concerns a specific activity and requires justified impact assessment and authorization.')]
